.deliberate/prompt_twofield_r3.txt:21:Project context: Rust + wgpu 29 + WGSL, WebGPU-portable (storage buffers 9/stage raisable to 16; no float atomics — fixed-point i32; no indirect dispatch). Prior baselines: main's MPM (converged Poisson too slow, divergence-penalty Darcy blocked infiltration, A≠D·G) and the XPBD/PBF rewrite (percolation/wetting/statics green; no impact momentum). Settled user decisions (do not re-litigate): full L0–L3 program in one plan; pure bet, NO fallback architecture; new solver only; ≤33 ms/frame @200k on Apple M5 as the L3 gate; U6 parallel with U5.
.deliberate/prompt_twofield_r3.txt:37:  - docs/plans/2026-06-08-001-feat-water-grain-impact-coupling-plan.md
.deliberate/prompt_twofield_r3.txt:44:Build a new grid-based solver under `src/solvers/` — a two-velocity-field, APIC-transfer, constraint-incompressible multiphase method — that handles all three target interactions in one method: water↔water (incompressible, with impact cavities), granular bed↔itself (Drucker-Prager elastoplasticity, angle of repose, tamping memory), and water↔bed with porosity (Darcy percolation at physical infiltration rates through a deformable, swelling bed, with pour craters). The work is structured as a validation ladder of rungs (L0 water → L1 bed → L2 percolation → L3 full coupling), each rung with quantitative exit gates. This is a pure bet on the novel architecture: a failed gate stops the ladder for redesign — there is no pre-committed fallback path.
.deliberate/prompt_twofield_r3.txt:53:- **rewrite (XPBD/PBF):** percolation, wetting/swelling (volume-conserving), and granular statics all work and are test-gated — but position projection derives velocity *after* the constraint solve, so impact momentum dies in the density projection. Both impact-coupling attempts (water→grain committed-dormant; water↔water reverted in `4dca52f`) confirmed there is no contact-pressure layer to patch. No craters, no cavities, no tamping memory.
