.deliberate/codex_review_twofield_r1.md:9:4. **Fine local Jacobi after a coarse seed is not obviously enough for visual incompressibility.** Fixed Jacobi smoothing damps high-frequency residual slowly for variable-coefficient and free-surface Poisson problems. The plan needs pre-registered absolute tolerances tied to visible density/free-surface error, plus a two-grid convergence factor gate on known eigenmodes. “≥2× per budget doubling” is weak; a solver can satisfy that while still requiring too many sweeps for 33 ms.
.deliberate/codex_review_twofield_r1.md:23:11. **The bed model is plausible but the performance premise is under-defended.** One 3x3 SVD per solid particle per substep in WGSL may be fine at 12k solids, but L3 says 200k particles without clearly bounding solid count, substeps, or plasticity frequency. Repose ≥30° is plausible with Drucker-Prager, but only if boundary friction, grid resolution, φ cap, and damping do not dominate the heap metric.
.deliberate/codex_review_twofield_r1.md:27:13. **Several gates still allow tuning to pass.** Cavity depth/persistence, pond depth/drain time, repose, and crater depth all depend on knobs that are not fully pre-registered. The knob-grid rule is good, but it must include pressure tolerance, coarse ratio, sweep count, timestep/substep cap, suction parameters, drag blend width, solids-pressure stiffness, plastic damping, and boundary/filter coefficients before implementation.
